pt09 adversarial lane. Surface: intake normalization / cue morphology.
RED: the preceding commit.

Seven regex literals across the engine spelled a contraction with the ASCII
apostrophe alone, and all seven are matched against `unit.text` — the reader's
own bytes, which intake deliberately does not rewrite. `normalizeText` and
`tokenize` both fold U+2019, so retrieval was never affected and no benchmark
could see it; only the cue layer reads raw text, and only the cue layer broke.

Each `'` joining two letters INSIDE a regex literal becomes ['|U+2019]. The
substitution is length-neutral in the text it matches and touches no quote
delimiter and no prose, so every index-based clause slice downstream is
unchanged.

The seven: MODAL cues (341), MISREADING_DENIAL_CUES (855),
QUOTED_ASSERTION_VERBS (907), CONTRADICTION_CUES (911), the sex-binary
overreach rule (979), the consent-safety overreach rule (1016), and the
retrieval disqualifier at 1106. The consent rule is the one worth naming:
`doesn't need consent` did not fire on the spelling most word processors emit.

Measured on the archived corpus: 0 of 1,039 segments move. The 17 curly
negation contractions present never sit where a cue decides a label — the
zero is what the corpus is, not evidence the engine was right. The test
guards the source, not one label, so the next cue written in ASCII alone
fails here.

Floors unchanged: domainRecall, ignorePrecision, junkRecall, knownSplits all
unmoved; no threshold crossings, so no rulings entered.

